<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: LicenseRef-BrowserAI-FSL-1.1-MIT-5yr -->
<Project>

  <!--
    THIS FILE IS THE FLOAT, NOT A PIN.

    Every dependency resolves to the latest release at build time and the build
    freezes what it resolved into the artifact. `Version="*"` is what performs
    that resolution; it is a floating version, and it excludes prerelease by
    construction, which is how DECISIONS.md's rule 3 ("GA is a hard floor") is
    enforced mechanically rather than by review.

    The resolved set is RECORDED rather than remembered, in packages.lock.json
    beside each project. That is a two-step operation, and the two steps are
    mutually exclusive in a single invocation: first a restore that forces
    re-evaluation, which re-resolves the float, then a locked-mode restore,
    which verifies what it resolved. With a lock file present and no forced
    re-evaluation, NuGet does not re-resolve and the float is silently dead
    (NU1512). A one-step locked-mode build is the failure this project exists
    to eliminate, wearing a safety helmet.

    The two commands verbatim are in DECISIONS.md, "The five rules that make
    floating safe". They are named rather than quoted here only because an XML
    comment cannot contain a double hyphen, which is what both flags begin with.

    Central management plus transitive pinning means this is the ONLY file in
    the repository where a package version may appear. A `Version=` attribute
    on a PackageReference anywhere else is a failing test in
    tests/BrowserAI.Tests/BuildConfigurationTests.cs, because a hard-coded
    version in a project file wins for that project while the lock file
    honestly records it: the artifact then contains a version nobody chose,
    and nothing says it is old.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
    <!--
      Without this, central package management REFUSES a floating version:
      NU1011, "The following PackageVersion items cannot specify a floating
      version". Measured 2026-08-16 on SDK 10.0.302, which is why it is here
      and not in the plan: floating is off by default under CPM, so the two
      properties above alone give a file that looks like the float and cannot
      restore. It is the enabling half of `Version="*"`, not a third opinion.
    -->
    <CentralPackageFloatingVersionsEnabled>true</CentralPackageFloatingVersionsEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Product">
    <!-- The MCP protocol implementation. Taken whole rather than as
         ModelContextProtocol.Core: the hosting dependency it carries is
         abstractions-only and does not drag in ASP.NET, and dropping to Core
         buys a smaller surface at the cost of AddMcpServer() and assembly
         scanning. Both halves of that trade are recorded in STACK.md.

         BrowserAI writes its own client AND server transports over this
         package's public TransportBase, because both of the SDK's own stdio
         transports are wrong for a proxy: the client one prepends `cmd.exe /c`
         and the server one hard-codes an encoder that escapes every backtick,
         apostrophe, angle bracket and non-ASCII character. See
         STACK.md deviations 1 and 5. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="ModelContextProtocol" Version="*" />
    <!-- Logging. The console provider is taken for one setting only:
         LogToStandardErrorThreshold, which is what forces every record to
         stderr by configuration rather than by rule. The file sink is ours,
         because the framework's queue behaviour at process exit is unverified
         and an unverified flush is the same class of assumption as an unread
         exit code. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" Version="*" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console" Version="*" />
    <!-- The update path (ARCHITECTURE.md, "Updates"). MIT, per-user install to
         %LocalAppData%, no elevation and no commercial tier, and the only
         option with both a stable executable path and a stage-now/swap-later
         primitive. It floats like everything else; the `vpk` CLI that produces
         the packages is a global tool and MUST be the same version, which is
         not something a lock file can enforce, so the release script checks it
         and refuses on a mismatch.

         NOTE FOR ANYONE EDITING THIS COMMENT: no double hyphen. One makes
         MSBuild fail to load this file, and restore then reports NU1015 "do not
         have a version specified" for EVERY package in the tree, naming central
         package management rather than the file that did not load. Hit while
         writing this comment on 2026-08-16, which is the fourth time this trap
         has cost this repository time. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Velopack" Version="*" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Analyzers">
    <!-- Turns BannedSymbols.txt into build errors. This is what makes "nothing
         anywhere in the process may reach the console" a mechanism instead of
         a habit that fails inside a catch. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers" Version="*" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Versioning">
    <!-- Reads the version out of the nearest git tag at build time, so no
         version number exists anywhere in this repository to edit, forget, or
         get out of step with the tag (STACK.md, "Versions come from git
         tags"). Build-time only, PrivateAssets="all", no runtime dependency
         and nothing for ILC to see.

         It floats like everything else. The repository this arrangement was
         taken from pins 6.0.0; that number was never looked up here and is
         not what restores. What restores is whatever is latest on the day, and
         the resolved version is recorded in packages.lock.json beside the
         projects. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="MinVer" Version="*" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Testing">
    <!-- TUnit: source-generated, reflection-free, MTP-native, MIT. It carries
         its own analyzers, which run at error severity (see .editorconfig)
         because an assertion that is not awaited passes silently.
         NEVER add FluentAssertions (commercial tier from 8.0.0).
         NEVER add Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk (TUnit is MTP-only; they conflict). -->
    <PackageVersion Include="TUnit" Version="*" />

    <!--
      THE LAYOUT ORACLE, AND IT IS TEST ONLY.

      Microsoft.Windows.CsWin32 generates Win32 declarations from Windows' own
      metadata. BrowserAI does NOT use it for its interop and the decision not
      to is recorded in TODO.md; it is referenced here for one job only, in
      tests/BrowserAI.Tests/InteropLayoutTests.cs: to obtain Microsoft's
      definition of the seven structs this product hand-writes, so their sizes
      and field offsets are checked against the vendor rather than against the
      last person who read the header.

      PrivateAssets="all" on the reference, exactly as MinVer does, so nothing
      reaches the product, nothing reaches ILC, and nothing acquires a
      redistribution obligation. NoticesTests asserts the second half.

      A LICENCE NOTE THAT MATTERS IF THIS EVER MOVES INTO THE PRODUCT: the
      generator is MIT, but the metadata packages it reads are not.
      Microsoft.Windows.SDK.Win32Metadata and WDK.Win32Metadata ship under
      Windows SDK licence terms and SDK.Win32Docs under aka.ms/WinSDKLicenseURL
      (checked on nuget.org 2026-08-17, re-verified 2026-08-19: all three carry
      no SPDX licence expression at all). Generated code, doc comments included,
      compiles into whatever references it. Test-only use sidesteps that question
      rather than answering it.

      The terms themselves are now in the repository, quoted verbatim with their
      URLs and the date they were fetched, in QUESTIONS.md section 12, along
      with the five questions they leave open and the fact that surfaced while
      gathering them: win32metadata's own README says Windows.Win32.winmd, the
      only file the generator reads, is MIT, while the package that delivers it
      declares the Windows 10 SDK EULA over the whole package and requires
      licence acceptance. Corrected 2026-08-19 (previously "and the question is
      NOT answered anywhere in this repository"). THE LEGAL QUESTION IS STILL NOT
      ANSWERED AND MUST NOT BE ASSERTED EITHER WAY. What changed is that it is
      now a question for a lawyer rather than a research task.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Windows.CsWin32" Version="*" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
